{#
mail/_thread_summary.html.twig
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
What a model made of this conversation, on request. Nothing here is spent
until somebody presses the button: there is no ingest hook, no job and no
cron, and a conversation nobody has asked about carries a control and nothing
else.

MACROS, NOT AN INCLUDE, because the feature is in two places on the page and
one Stimulus controller has to own both. The control sits on the right of the
subject line, where it is there for every conversation; the card sits below
the insight strip, where it is only there once there is something in it. A
controller reaches targets inside its own element only, so its element has to
be an ANCESTOR OF BOTH — the thread's content wrapper in _thread_content —
and `root()` is what stamps it there. Three insertion points, three macros:

  root(thread)             the controller and its values, inside the wrapper tag
  trigger(thread, summary) the control, for the subject row
  card(thread, summary)    the card, for below the insight strip

Each states the same gate for itself rather than trusting the caller to: a
page that got one of the three wrong would put a control on a conversation
that has nowhere to put an answer, or a card with nothing to fill it.

NO CARD UNTIL THERE IS SOMETHING TO PUT IN IT
─────────────────────────────────────────────
"Summary" written over an empty box is a box claiming a summary exists. So a
conversation that has never been summarised renders the card into a
<template>: markup the browser parses and then does not render, keeps out of
the accessibility tree, and does not return from querySelectorAll. The
controller swaps the template for its own contents at the first click — see
#mount() — which is also what puts the card exactly where this file said it
goes, without the controller knowing anything about the page around it.

The alternative was building the card in JavaScript, which would put this
markup, these classes and two translated strings in a second place that can
rot away from this one.

Accepts (all three macros):
  thread   MessageThread             the open conversation
  summary  StoredThreadSummary|null  what is already stored for it, or null.
           `isFresh` is false when the conversation has changed since it was
           written — which is SHOWN, greyed, with the regenerate offer beside
           it, rather than hidden. A summary of a thread that has gained one
           "thanks" is still mostly true, and hiding it makes the half-minute
           somebody already waited feel wasted.

A STORED SUMMARY IS THERE AT FIRST PAINT. That is the whole point of storing
it: reopening a summarised conversation must cost no model call and show no
gap, so `summary is not null` renders the card itself rather than a template.

Nothing renders at all when the feature is off for this person (either switch
— see ai_summary_enabled()) or when the conversation is a single message. A
control that is always refused is worse than no control, and a "summary" of
one message costs half a minute of GPU to say something reading the message
says faster. `thread.summary.too_short` stays reachable for the case a page
cannot see coming: a thread that had two messages when it was rendered and has
one by the time the button is pressed, which the endpoint refuses and the
controller says in words.

MOTION: the card is STILL when it renders a stored summary and animates only
when one arrives because somebody asked. _thread_content states the contract
this follows — "chrome that flinched every time a conversation opened would be
movement that says nothing" — and a summary that was already there is chrome.
One that has just been written is a genuine arrival, so the copy inside the
template — the only copy that can ever ARRIVE — is the one carrying data-enter.
#}

{#
The controller, its endpoint and its vocabulary, stamped inside the tag of
whatever element hosts the feature.

It is deliberately not the card's own tag any more: the card is the one part
of this that does not exist yet when the controller has to be listening.
#}
{% macro root(thread) %}
    {% if ai_summary_enabled() and thread.messageCount > 1 %}
        data-controller="mail--thread-summary"
        data-mail--thread-summary-url-value="{{ path('app_mail_thread_summary', {'id': thread.id}) }}"
        {# A per-action token, stamped here because assets/csrf.js reads the
           `csrf-token` meta tag and that tag carries the shared `ajax` one.
           The precedent is settings/account_order_controller, which csrf.js's
           own docblock names as the caller left alone on purpose. #}
        data-mail--thread-summary-token-value="{{ csrf_token('thread_summary') }}"
        data-mail--thread-summary-sent-label-value="{{ 'thread.summary.state.sent'|trans }}"
        data-mail--thread-summary-waiting-label-value="{{ 'thread.summary.state.waiting'|trans }}"
        data-mail--thread-summary-working-label-value="{{ 'thread.summary.state.working'|trans }}"
        data-mail--thread-summary-stopped-label-value="{{ 'thread.summary.state.stopped'|trans }}"
        data-mail--thread-summary-regenerate-label-value="{{ 'thread.summary.regenerate'|trans }}"
        data-mail--thread-summary-failed-label-value="{{ 'thread.summary.error.generic'|trans }}"
        data-mail--thread-summary-no-answer-label-value="{{ 'thread.summary.error.no_answer'|trans }}"
        data-mail--thread-summary-disabled-label-value="{{ 'thread.summary.error.disabled'|trans }}"
        data-mail--thread-summary-timeout-label-value="{{ 'thread.summary.error.timeout'|trans }}"
        data-mail--thread-summary-unreachable-label-value="{{ 'thread.summary.error.unreachable'|trans }}"
        data-mail--thread-summary-model-missing-label-value="{{ 'thread.summary.error.model_missing'|trans }}"
        data-mail--thread-summary-too-short-label-value="{{ 'thread.summary.too_short'|trans }}"
    {% endif %}
{% endmacro %}

{#
The control, and it is the ONLY one: Summarise, Summarise again and Stop are
the same control in the same place in every state, because a button that moved
into the card once the card existed would be a button somebody has to find
twice.

<button>, not an anchor, and that is load-bearing: _reply_zone.html.twig
records what an anchor costs here — "Turbo Drive prefetches a link on hover,
and hovering Reply is what you do on the way to clicking it". A hover that
spent half a minute of GPU would be the same bug with a much larger bill.

ml-auto, in a row aligned on the baseline: the pill sits at the right-hand end
of the subject line and its label sits on the subject's own baseline, which is
what keeps it reading as part of that line rather than as a second row.
#}
{% macro trigger(thread, summary) %}
    {% if ai_summary_enabled() and thread.messageCount > 1 %}
        <div class="ml-auto flex shrink-0 items-center gap-1">
            <button type="button"
                    data-mail--thread-summary-target="run"
                    data-action="click->mail--thread-summary#{{ summary is null ? 'run' : 'regenerate' }}"
                    class="flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-full border border-line px-2.5 py-1
                           text-xs font-medium whitespace-nowrap text-ink-muted
                           hover:bg-hover hover:text-ink transition-colors cursor-pointer">
                <i class="fa-solid fa-wand-magic-sparkles" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                {# The label is the accessible name — no aria-label beside it,
                   because the controller rewrites this text when a run
                   finishes and a second copy of the name would go stale. #}
                <span data-mail--thread-summary-target="runLabel"
                >{{ (summary is null ? 'thread.summary.button' : 'thread.summary.regenerate')|trans }}</span>
            </button>

            <button type="button"
                    data-mail--thread-summary-target="stop"
                    hidden
                    data-action="click->mail--thread-summary#stop"
                    class="rounded-full border border-line px-2.5 py-1 text-xs font-medium
                           whitespace-nowrap text-ink-muted
                           hover:bg-hover hover:text-ink transition-colors cursor-pointer">
                {{ 'thread.summary.stop'|trans }}
            </button>
        </div>
    {% endif %}
{% endmacro %}

{#
The card — the answer, and nothing else. Written once and then either rendered
or parked in a <template>, so the copy that arrives on a click and the copy
that is there on reopening cannot drift apart.
#}
{% macro card(thread, summary) %}
    {% if ai_summary_enabled() and thread.messageCount > 1 %}
        {% set isStale = summary is not null and summary.isFresh == false %}

        {% set markup %}
            <div class="mb-4"
                 data-mail--thread-summary-target="card"
                 {# Only the parked copy can ever ARRIVE; the rendered one was
                    always there. See the MOTION note above. #}
                 {% if summary is null %}data-enter="fade"{% endif %}
            >
                <p class="mb-1.5 text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-ink-faint">
                    {{ 'thread.summary.heading'|trans }}
                </p>

                {# The insight card's chrome, which is the card shape this pane
                   already has. The mail-sheet utility re-points every colour
                   token, so a card inside it inherits sheet colours for free. #}
                <div class="rounded-pane border border-line bg-surface px-3 py-2.5 shadow-sm"
                     data-thread-summary>

                    <div class="flex items-start gap-2">
                        {# Something moving while nothing is arriving. Between
                           the click and the first token there can be forty
                           seconds — eighteen of a 30B model coming off disk and
                           twenty-three of the conversation being read — and
                           Ollama reports no progress through any of it, so
                           there is nothing to fill a bar with and a still
                           sentence reads as a hung card. aria-hidden: the
                           status line beside it already announces the state in
                           words. Held still under prefers-reduced-motion, where
                           it stays three plain dots. #}
                        <span data-mail--thread-summary-target="pending"
                              hidden
                              aria-hidden="true"
                              class="ai-pending mt-[5px] shrink-0"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>

                        <div class="min-w-0 flex-1">
                            {# `role="status"` with aria-live so the STATE
                               TRANSITIONS are announced rather than only drawn.
                               Hidden while empty so a finished summary carries
                               no furniture above it. The controller waits a
                               task after mounting this before it writes the
                               first sentence — a live region inserted with its
                               text already in it announces nothing. #}
                            <p data-mail--thread-summary-target="status"
                               role="status"
                               aria-live="polite"
                               hidden
                               class="text-[11px] leading-snug text-ink-muted"></p>

                            {# The staleness notice, which only ever goes away:
                               pressing regenerate clears it by starting a run. #}
                            <p data-mail--thread-summary-target="stale"
                               {{ isStale ? '' : 'hidden' }}
                               class="text-[11px] leading-snug text-ink-muted"
                            >{{ 'thread.summary.stale'|trans }}</p>

                            {# "It did not see all of this."

                               A separate line from the staleness one, and both
                               can be on at once, because they are different
                               warnings about the same paragraph: stale means
                               the thread moved on AFTER this was written,
                               partial means the model was never shown the whole
                               of it in the first place. Regenerating fixes the
                               first and cannot fix the second, so collapsing
                               them into one sentence would offer a button that
                               does nothing about half of what it claims.

                               Unlike the staleness notice this one does NOT
                               only ever go away: a regenerate on a thread that
                               is still too long is still partial, so the
                               controller sets it from the run's own answer. #}
                            <p data-mail--thread-summary-target="partial"
                               {{ summary is not null and summary.isPartial ? '' : 'hidden' }}
                               class="text-[11px] leading-snug text-ink-muted"
                            >{{ 'thread.summary.partial'|trans }}</p>

                            {# A LIST, because three findings in a block of
                               prose is three findings nobody separates while
                               skimming — which is the only way a summary is
                               ever read.

                               The model is asked for one short line per point
                               and NO bullet characters: the marker and the
                               hanging indent are the card's, so a wrapped line
                               aligns under its own first word instead of under
                               a dash. A leading dash is stripped anyway, since
                               older stored summaries were written when the
                               prompt asked for one and a model can always
                               oblige unasked.

                               THE SAME SPLIT LIVES IN mail--thread-summary's
                               #output(), which rebuilds this list token by
                               token while a summary is being written. Two
                               implementations of one rule, in two languages,
                               and they have to agree: change one, change the
                               other. The alternative was leaving the streamed
                               copy as a block of text and rewriting it into a
                               list at the end, which is a different shape on
                               screen at the moment the reader is already
                               reading it.

                               The greying is applied server-side to a stale
                               copy and removed by the controller at the first
                               token of a new run. #}
                            <ul data-mail--thread-summary-target="output"
                                {{ summary is null ? 'hidden' : '' }}
                                class="flex flex-col gap-1.5 list-disc pl-4 text-sm leading-relaxed
                                       text-ink marker:text-ink-faint
                                       {{ isStale ? ' opacity-60' : '' }}"
                            >
                                {% for raw in (summary is null ? '' : summary.text)|split('\n') %}
                                    {% set line = raw|trim %}
                                    {# LEADING marker only, and not a `replace`:
                                       that would take the dash out of "Backend
                                       / Python – Cara Care" too, which is a
                                       sentence the model was right to write.

                                       Flat rather than a loop over the markers,
                                       because Twig scopes `set` to the `for`
                                       that encloses it — a marker loop would
                                       strip the dash and then hand the untouched
                                       line to the `li` below. Every marker here
                                       is one character and a space, so one
                                       slice serves them all. #}
                                    {% if line starts with '– ' or line starts with '— '
                                        or line starts with '- ' or line starts with '* '
                                        or line starts with '• ' %}
                                        {% set line = line|slice(2)|trim %}
                                    {% endif %}
                                    {% if line is not empty %}
                                        <li>{{ line }}</li>
                                    {% endif %}
                                {% endfor %}
                            </ul>

                            {% if summary is not null and summary.writtenAt is not null %}
                                <p class="mt-1 text-[11px] text-ink-faint">
                                    {{ 'thread.summary.generated'|trans({
                                        '%when%': summary.writtenAt|intl_date('dMMM') ~ ', ' ~ summary.writtenAt|date(clock.time),
                                    }) }}
                                </p>
                            {% endif %}

                            <p class="mt-1 text-[11px] leading-snug text-ink-faint">
                                {{ 'thread.summary.disclaimer'|trans }}
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        {% endset %}

        {% if summary is null %}
            <template data-mail--thread-summary-target="cardTemplate">{{ markup }}</template>
        {% else %}
            {{ markup }}
        {% endif %}
    {% endif %}
{% endmacro %}
